00:00:06.057 --> 00:00:07.640
RACHEL DEACON-SMITH:
Today we're going

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.250
to give you a very quick
talk on BigQuery GIS.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.560
So, looking at the
agenda, we'll give you

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.650
a very brief introduction
into BigQuery GIS So,

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:17.880
what exactly is it?

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.258
And some of the data
types around it.

00:00:20.258 --> 00:00:22.050
And then we will spend
the bulk of the time

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.450
giving you guys a demo,
so showcasing the features

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:28.650
and showing you some of
the example use cases.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.990
You have three speakers
today, Kannappan, Thinh,

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:33.450
and myself, Rachel.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.310
We are all a strategic
cloud engineers

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.610
based in the London office.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.410
OK, so BigQuery.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:45.990
For those that do not know,
BigQuery is Google Cloud's data

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:47.980
enterprise data warehouse.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.890
It can manage large data.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:53.148
So, we manage storage
on the petabyte scale

00:00:53.148 --> 00:00:55.440
and you can run analysis on
up to billions and billions

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.480
of growths.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.900
You can interact with
BigQuery using SQL.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.592
And it's fully managed
and serverless,

00:01:02.592 --> 00:01:04.800
so you don't have to worry
about spinning up clusters

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.010
and managing resources.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:07.300
That's all done for you.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.850
The idea that you load the
data, run your analysis.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:13.210
It's super big,
it's super quick.

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.390
So then we move on
to BigQuery GIS.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:19.290
So GIS is Geospatial
Information Systems.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.050
And BigQuery is one of
the first cloud providers

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:26.790
to offer an MPP, massively
parallel processing data

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:30.240
warehouse that supports
geospatial functions and data

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.410
types.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:35.640
So we have the concept
of a geography data type,

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.390
and that's essentially a point
set on the Earth's surface.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.460
And when you combine that
with your location data

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:42.487
in your BigQuery table--

00:01:42.487 --> 00:01:44.070
so, for example, if
you have longitude

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.960
and latitude or country codes--

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.090
you can essentially derive
really powerful insight.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.212
It makes your data
lot more relatable.

00:01:51.212 --> 00:01:52.920
Underpinning this all
is the same library

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.810
used in Google Maps.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:58.945
And we also have the
BigQuery visualization tool

00:01:58.945 --> 00:02:00.570
which is a lightweight
tool that allows

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:05.110
you to view your analysis
against a live map.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:08.047
So I mentioned the
geography data type.

00:02:08.047 --> 00:02:09.630
And I said this
represents a point set

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.230
on the Earth's surface.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.680
So by points set this can
be an actual like point

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.960
on the surface or a set
of points, so a line

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.590
string or a polygon.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:20.520
And we'll see this in
action in the demo.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.930
You also have GIS
functions and types.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.350
And if you're
familiar with PostGIS

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:26.740
you'll be familiar with this.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.157
And these essentially
just operate

00:02:28.157 --> 00:02:31.320
on the geography data type.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:32.370
Enough talking, I think.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:34.683
Let's hand over to
Kanna, who will do a demo

00:02:34.683 --> 00:02:36.600
and show you some of
these features in action.

00:02:42.907 --> 00:02:43.990
KANNAPPAN SIRCHABESAN: OK.

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:45.360
Hi, welcome everyone.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.480
So I'm doing a demo
of, like the data

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.250
types and the functions that are
available within BigQuery GIS.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.720
So this, like we are
starting with a blank slate.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:59.370
So on the left side, you have
the query and on the right side

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:00.250
you have the map.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:03.480
So this is like a visualization
tool as part of BigQuery.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.020
So it's got query Geo Viz.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.840
So what we are doing
now is, basically, we

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:10.030
are starting from scratch.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.400
So we are drawing a
point of where we are.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.160
So we are in Moscone Center.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.230
So, the function we use is
s t underscore geogpoint

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:23.260
and we provide the lat and long.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:28.200
So, as you can see, we have the
Moscone Center on the right.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:34.480
So, yeah, this is one point.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.540
Now we are going to
draw one more point.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:42.380
So this is the baseball
stadium, Oracle Park.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.300
So, as you can
see, like, the s t

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.480
underscore geogpoint
takes like two parameters.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.200
The longitude first and then the
latitude, just the convention.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.240
So what you see now
is, basically, you

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:58.960
have, like both the points
using the union hall method.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.120
And now we are going to
connect both the points using

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:05.800
s t underscore makeline.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.050
And I do yonan all again
so that I display the line

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:11.460
on the dot on the two points.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:12.390
OK.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:17.310
Now we are going to draw like
a polygon of three points,

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:20.200
using Google SF office
as the third point.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.825
And what we use here is s
t underscore makepolygon.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:28.500
And we use the job
from text function.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.120
And then we draw a polygon.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.050
OK, once we have
the polygon, now we

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:39.820
can do all the other measures
like distance perimeter,

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:40.420
and so on.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:42.810
So what I've shown
here is that centroid.

00:04:45.482 --> 00:04:46.940
What you're seeing
now is basically

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:52.070
the public data sets that are
available within BigQuery.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:58.280
So the one that is shown here
is basically the bike data set,

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.680
the Ford GoBike.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:03.910
So these are the bike
stations that are available.

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:05.910
So as we can see, there
are like three clusters.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.583
So this is the cluster
in San Francisco.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.340
So these are all
the bike stations.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.320
Now let's filter to
the bike stations

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.800
that are within that specific
polygon, using the function

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:23.770
s t within.

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:26.710
S t stands for
spatial types which is

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:31.597
a common terminology in geog.

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:33.680
So, as you can see, there
also like a street view.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:36.210
So I just clicked on
one of the points.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.240
And as you can see,
you can see the Ford

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.340
go bike station with the bikes.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.460
So this is like a
complex polygon.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:51.040
So it's basically the zip code.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.100
So you can get the zip code data
set, your zip code data set,

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.350
from the BigQuery
public data sets.

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:07.420
So what we see now here is--

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:18.195
So this is like, all
the bike stations.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:36.960
So yeah, this is like
the cluster of points.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:40.790
So what this cluster represents
is the size of the circle,

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:42.860
represents the
number of bikes that

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:45.230
are available within that
specific bike station.

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:47.000
So, if the circle
is bigger, which

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.730
means that there are a lot
of bikes, and if it's smaller

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:53.260
it means it's a less
number of bikes.

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:56.810
And what you're going to see
now is basically from Moscone

00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:00.260
within a 500 meter radius
what other bike stations

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.007
that are available.

00:07:02.007 --> 00:07:03.590
So these are some
of the bike stations

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:06.700
that are available within
a 500 meter distance.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:08.060
Find a meter radius.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:11.000
So, as you can see, that
is one very close by,

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:18.420
in front of like the Whole
Foods just right outside.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:18.920
OK?

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:20.337
So, as you can
see, it's like very

00:07:20.337 --> 00:07:26.270
easy to actually do all this
like basic methods and data

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:27.180
types.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:30.350
So you can start using
it to do very complex

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.750
geospatial functionalities.

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:34.070
Thank you.

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:35.070
THINH HA: Thanks, Kanna.

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.163
So, for my demo,
I'm going to talk

00:07:40.163 --> 00:07:42.330
