00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:48.798
Keeping with the
cycle higher data set,

00:07:48.798 --> 00:07:50.340
I'm going to move
across the Atlantic

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.380
to London where we're based.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:56.520
I'm going to also take the view
of the operator of the cycle

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.090
highway service.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:01.770
So, as operator, I want to know
what do people use the cycle

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.480
service for.

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:07.650
And I like my drawing
the 50 most common path

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:09.840
that's taken by my service.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:15.690
I can see that most of the path
is happening around Hyde Park.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:17.580
So Hyde Park is a big station.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.190
It's a big park
in central London

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:21.930
that's very popular
with tourists.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.630
What this tells me is that
people are using this service

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:25.905
quite a lot for tourism.

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:32.450
So, looking at the
previous analysis,

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:35.240
I can see that some stations
are more frequently used

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.650
as a destination i.e.
more bikes arrive

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:41.270
at that attestation than no
bike starting from that station.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:46.670
So this may cause some
capacity problems.

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:48.770
If I'm a tourist arriving
at the station that's

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:52.140
very frequently used, I might
not find a docking station.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:54.560
So I want to do some
analysis of this trend.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:58.340
What I've done here is I've
counted the number of trips

00:08:58.340 --> 00:09:00.290
that started at the
station and subtracted it

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:02.560
from the number of trips
that end at that station,

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:03.765
and plotted on a map.

00:09:03.765 --> 00:09:05.390
Red means there are
more trips starting

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:07.380
from that station
and blue means there

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:09.320
are more trips ending
at that station.

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:12.380
Looking at the station
with the biggest disparity,

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:14.210
I can see that corresponds
to hop exchange

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:17.000
to Borough, which
is where I know

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:18.860
there's a big farmer's
market that's also

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:20.510
very popular with tourists.

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:22.580
So, with this
knowledge, I can start

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:26.330
making some changes to
accommodate for this trend.

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:28.530
This also make me
ask the question,

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:32.160
do people use this cycle
service for commute at all?

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:35.150
So, by isolating
the trips to hours

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.370
in the morning versus
evening commute,

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:40.910
I can immediately see a
trend where in the morning

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.280
most people are using
the service to commute

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:46.280
into central London and
vise versa in the evening.

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:49.520
This is most pronounced
at King's Cross Station,

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:51.770
which you might have
heard from Harry Potter.

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:54.980
It's also a big
commute spot where

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:59.030
for everybody outside
of London commuting in.

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:02.570
So, personally, I also like
to go to Borough Market

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:04.030
in the weekend.

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:06.550
I know that I won't be
able to dock my bike.

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:09.180
That's a very
common destination.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:15.280
So what I can do is I can find
the 10 closest station nearby

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:17.030
and immediately I can
see that the closest

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:18.860
station is Park Street
bank side, which

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:21.410
is about 164 meters away.

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:21.910
Thank you.

00:10:26.588 --> 00:10:27.880
RACHEL DEACON-SMITH: Thank you.

